1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,188
SELinux is the security guard inside Linux.

2
00:00:03,208 --> 00:00:07,629
Many people meet it for the first time when something stops working,

3
00:00:07,649 --> 00:00:11,482
and they switch it off. Don't. Learn the two modes instead:

4
00:00:11,502 --> 00:00:13,180
enforcing, and permissive.

5
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:17,683
In this video: set enforcing and permissive modes for SELinux.

6
00:00:22,203 --> 00:00:24,786
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

7
00:00:25,256 --> 00:00:28,286
Set enforcing and permissive modes for SELinux.

8
00:00:28,756 --> 00:00:31,965
We'll read the current mode, change it until the next reboot,

9
00:00:31,985 --> 00:00:34,347
change it for the next boot, and put it back.

10
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:43,521
SELinux has three modes.

11
00:00:43,541 --> 00:00:48,126
In enforcing, SELinux blocks what the policy forbids, and logs it.

12
00:00:48,146 --> 00:00:51,475
In permissive, it only logs, and blocks nothing:

13
00:00:51,495 --> 00:00:56,568
perfect for finding the cause of a problem. And disabled: SELinux is off.

14
00:00:56,588 --> 00:01:01,452
Avoid disabled: turning it on again needs a relabel of the whole disk.

15
00:01:05,922 --> 00:01:08,498
There are two places to set the mode.

16
00:01:08,518 --> 00:01:14,249
The command setenforce changes the running system, and is lost at the next reboot.

17
00:01:14,269 --> 00:01:17,897
It can only switch between enforcing and permissive.

18
00:01:17,917 --> 00:01:21,474
The file /etc/selinux/config sets the mode at boot.

19
00:01:21,494 --> 00:01:24,420
For a permanent change, you edit the file.

20
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:38,827
First, the current mode. getenforce prints it in one word: Enforcing.

21
00:01:38,847 --> 00:01:44,231
Then grep shows the mode for the next boot, in the file /etc/selinux/config:

22
00:01:44,251 --> 00:01:45,511
SELINUX=enforcing.

23
00:01:45,531 --> 00:01:50,702
The single quotes keep the shell away from the text, and the caret means:

24
00:01:50,722 --> 00:01:52,480
at the start of the line.

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,471
sestatus shows more. The status is enabled. The policy is targeted.

26
00:02:01,491 --> 00:02:06,700
The current mode is enforcing, and the mode from the config file is enforcing:

27
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:11,593
two lines, two places. If they differ, the mode changes at the next boot.

28
00:02:16,113 --> 00:02:21,545
Now change the mode, until the next reboot. setenforce 0 means permissive.

29
00:02:21,565 --> 00:02:26,554
getenforce confirms: Permissive. setenforce 1 means enforcing again.

30
00:02:26,574 --> 00:02:31,858
And getenforce says Enforcing. This needs sudo: only root can change it.

31
00:02:36,378 --> 00:02:41,860
Now the next boot. We edit the file with sed. The option -i edits the file in place.

32
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,873
The text between the quotes is a substitution:

33
00:02:44,893 --> 00:02:48,214
s, slash, the old text, slash, the new text, slash.

34
00:02:48,234 --> 00:02:51,554
The old text is any line that starts with SELINUX=.

35
00:02:51,574 --> 00:02:55,353
The new text is SELINUX=permissive. grep shows the result.

36
00:02:55,373 --> 00:02:57,711
But getenforce still says Enforcing:

37
00:02:57,731 --> 00:03:01,510
the file acts at the next boot, not on the running system.

38
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:10,466
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

39
00:03:11,986 --> 00:03:16,425
We reboot. And getenforce says Permissive: the file was read at boot.

40
00:03:16,445 --> 00:03:21,013
sestatus, with a pipe to grep, keeps only the lines with the word mode.

41
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:23,017
The current mode is permissive.

42
00:03:27,537 --> 00:03:31,149
Back to normal. The same sed command, with enforcing.

43
00:03:31,169 --> 00:03:34,575
Then setenforce 1, so we do not wait for a reboot.

44
00:03:34,595 --> 00:03:38,344
getenforce says Enforcing, and the file says enforcing.

45
00:03:38,364 --> 00:03:41,085
Always leave a system in enforcing mode.

46
00:03:46,605 --> 00:03:52,376
Your turn. Set SELinux to permissive now, with setenforce, and for the next boot too,

47
00:03:52,396 --> 00:03:57,759
in the file /etc/selinux/config. Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

48
00:03:57,779 --> 00:04:02,052
On your own system, put it back to enforcing when you are done.

49
00:04:12,522 --> 00:04:14,272
Try before you open the solution.

50
00:04:19,792 --> 00:04:24,252
Next up: list and identify SELinux file and process context.

51
00:04:24,722 --> 00:04:27,625
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
